From be69fef61d325bdd9c03d640cd3796903352fdad Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: iitimii <timiiowolabi@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 08:59:19 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 29/31] S3.3: staged startup - mlocked front first, background
 tier loader

The main thread now loads only the front tier (mmap+mlock under its default
POL_MLOCK policy, the load-time half of tier policy) and starts answering as
soon as the verdict tokens resolve. One background thread loads easy then mid,
serialized, publishing each with a release store under a mutex plus a cv
notify; consumers call tier_acquire(), which blocks only where the mode really
needs that tier.

The router prefers overlap to blocking: easy still loading -> the front answers
conf-monitored (no wait); mid still loading on a hard route -> the front keeps
extending its TTFT opener with committed, seam-cut segments until mid turns
READY, then hands off exactly as before. The extension budget is
min(3*seg_max, n_predict/2) front tokens for the whole turn, opener included:
the plan's 3*seg_max cap alone let the front consume all 128 tokens of a
-n 128 answer and hand the routed expert a zero budget. Codraft and verify
wait, since neither has a useful degraded mode.

Adds TS_FAILED so a failed background load wakes its waiters instead of
hanging them, [tier]/[ttft] timing traces (G11's measurement hooks), and a
join-before-teardown discipline: the explicit join covers the normal path and
duo_state's last member is a joiner whose destructor covers every early return.
Two races the second thread would otherwise introduce are closed with it: a
jtrace() line is now atomic as a line, and the -q log callback's CONT-level
carry is atomic rather than a plain static.

Assisted-by: Claude Fable 5
